  "answer": "**Yes: an update naming the same tag re-pulls it and rebuilds the snapshot.** V1 \u00b7 PUBLIC: tag moved, no update: new sessions ran build A-r1027191511; the same-tag update (v2, READY in 6 s) \u2192 new sessions ran build B-r1027191511; an update with nothing changed still created v3 (READY in 6 s). V1 \u00b7 VPC: tag moved, no update: new sessions ran build A-r1027191511; the same-tag update (v2, READY in 16 s) \u2192 new sessions ran build B-r1027191511; an update with nothing changed still created v3 (READY in 16 s). V2 \u00b7 PUBLIC: with the tag moved but no update, new sessions still restored build A-r1027191511; the same-tag update (v2, READY in 3.1 min) resolved the tag again (ECR pulls: tag ci \u00d73, digest B-r1027191511 \u00d72); new sessions ran build B-r1027191511 from a newly built snapshot; an update with nothing changed still created v3 (READY in 3.1 min) and rebuilt the snapshot. V2 \u00b7 VPC: with the tag moved but no update, new sessions still restored build A-r1027191511; the same-tag update (v2, READY in 3.4 min) resolved the tag again (ECR pulls: tag ci \u00d73, digest B-r1027191511 \u00d72); new sessions ran build B-r1027191511 from a newly built snapshot; an update with nothing changed still created v3 (READY in 3.4 min) and rebuilt the snapshot. (Build A `A-r1027191511`, build B `B-r1027191511`.)"
 },
